# Copy to .env.local for local dev. Values below match docker-compose.yml so the
# local stack works out of the box. Prod sets these in the host's env (e.g. Vercel).

# --- Database (Postgres) -----------------------------------------------------
# Local: the docker-compose Postgres. Prod: Neon (or any Postgres).
# NOTE: use 127.0.0.1, not localhost — some setups resolve localhost to IPv6 ::1
# while the mapped port is IPv4-only.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://papervine:papervine@127.0.0.1:5432/papervine

# --- Platform auth (Better Auth, SPEC §10.1) ---------------------------------
# Generate a secret: openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=replace-me-with-a-random-secret
# The app's own origin — must EXACTLY match where the dashboard is served (scheme +
# host, no trailing slash), or Better Auth rejects sign-in with "Invalid origin".
# The control plane serves on the apex, so: local dev http://localhost:3000,
# prod https://papervine.io.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Platform superadmin allowlist (SPEC §10.10): comma-separated emails whose accounts can
# open the cross-tenant /admin overview on the app host. Unset → the surface stays dark
# (404) for everyone. Case-insensitive.
# PLATFORM_ADMIN_EMAILS=you@example.com
# Google sign-in for the dashboard — OPTIONAL. Unset, the provider is absent and the
# "Continue with Google" button never renders (email + password keeps working). Set BOTH
# halves to enable it, and register this exact Authorized redirect URI on the OAuth client:
#   {BETTER_AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/google
# That's the APEX origin, not the app. host — Google refuses redirect URIs on a subdomain of
# localhost, so the middleware forwards the callback to the app host. See docs/auth/google-sign-in.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=...apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Transactional email (Resend, SPEC §10.1) ---------------------------------
# Powers email verification, password reset, and invitation delivery. OPTIONAL, and what
# "unset" means depends on where you run:
#   - outside production: messages are LOGGED TO THE SERVER CONSOLE, link included. A real
#     transport — every flow works, which is what lets local dev, CI, and the e2e suite run
#     with no vendor account.
#   - in production: the inbox-dependent flows switch OFF (/forgot-password says reset is
#     unavailable, the login page hides its link), because promising "check your inbox" for a
#     message that only reached a log file lies to a real user.
# Invitations always work via the Copy-link UI in Members settings either way.
# EMAIL_FROM must be on a domain verified in Resend or every send is rejected.
# Swapping providers means replacing one `deliver` call in src/lib/email.ts.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# EMAIL_FROM=Papervine <hello@example.com>

# --- Object storage (S3 API, SPEC §3.1 / §11) -------------------------------
# Local: the docker-compose MinIO. Prod: Cloudflare R2 (endpoint = your R2 URL).
# Again 127.0.0.1, not localhost, for the local endpoint.
S3_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:9000
S3_REGION=auto
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=papervine
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=papervinesecret
S3_BUCKET=papervine-content

# --- Realtime (Pusher protocol, SPEC §10.3) ----------------------------------
# Drives the live Activity feed: it updates the instant a sync starts/finishes instead of
# waiting for the poll. STRICTLY OPTIONAL — unset, the feed falls back to polling (today's
# behavior), so CI and a bare checkout work without it.
# Local: the docker-compose Soketi (values below match its SOKETI_DEFAULT_APP_*). Prod:
# hosted Pusher Channels — set PUSHER_CLUSTER and drop PUSHER_HOST/PORT/USE_TLS.
# The PUSHER_KEY (server) and NEXT_PUBLIC_PUSHER_KEY (browser) must be the SAME value.
# PUSHER_APP_ID=papervine
# PUSHER_KEY=papervine-key
# PUSHER_SECRET=papervine-secret
# PUSHER_HOST=127.0.0.1
# PUSHER_PORT=6001
# PUSHER_USE_TLS=false
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PUSHER_KEY=papervine-key
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PUSHER_HOST=127.0.0.1
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PUSHER_PORT=6001
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PUSHER_USE_TLS=false
# Hosted Pusher Channels (prod) instead of HOST/PORT/USE_TLS:
# PUSHER_CLUSTER=us3
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PUSHER_CLUSTER=us3

# --- Collaborative editing (SPEC §9.2) ---------------------------------------
# Standalone Hocuspocus (Yjs) socket for real-time co-editing. Unset → the editor falls back to
# same-browser BroadcastChannel sync (still works, just not across machines). To enable:
#   1) COLLAB_JWT_SECRET — shared secret the Next app signs room tokens with and the collab
#      service verifies (must MATCH the value in docker-compose / the deployed service).
#   2) NEXT_PUBLIC_COLLAB_URL — the browser's WebSocket URL for the service.
# Local (docker-compose `collab` service): ws://127.0.0.1:1234. Prod: wss://collab.<your-domain>.
# COLLAB_JWT_SECRET=papervine-collab-dev-secret
# NEXT_PUBLIC_COLLAB_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:1234

# --- AI (assistant, editor agent, automations — SPEC §8/§18) ------------------
# Model AND route are configuration, not code (src/lib/ai-model.ts). They always travel
# together, so pick ONE profile below and uncomment it. Leave everything commented and
# the AI surfaces degrade cleanly — they return 503 with "not configured" and the rest of
# the product works normally.
#
# ---- Profile A · Local, free (recommended for development) -------------------
# Runs on your own machine, costs nothing, unlimited iteration — ideal for working on
# the *mechanics* (does a run enqueue, draft, publish, meter, appear in history?).
# Needs Ollama (or LM Studio) running with a tool-capable model pulled:
#     ollama pull qwen3    OR    docker compose --profile local-ai up -d
# Caveat: small models are wobbly at the multi-step tool use our agents rely on, so
# check output QUALITY on a hosted profile before trusting it. Docs: /self-hosting/local-ai
# AI_ROUTING=direct
# PAPERVINE_AI_MODEL=ollama/qwen3
# AI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1   # only to override the default endpoint
# AI_LOCAL_API_KEY=                        # only if your server demands one
# AI_LOCAL_REASONING=1                    # let a thinking model think (SLOW on a laptop:
#                                         # 40s vs 1.9s for one answer — off by default)
#
# ---- Profile B · Vercel AI Gateway (one key, every provider) -----------------
# The simplest hosted option: a single key unlocks nearly every provider+model, so you
# can switch models without touching code or adding SDKs. COSTS MONEY per token.
# Key from the Vercel dashboard → AI Gateway; on Vercel deployments OIDC is automatic.
# Long-lived workers (the Trigger.dev executor) need the real key, not OIDC.
# AI_ROUTING=gateway
# PAPERVINE_AI_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5
# AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=
#
# ---- Profile C · Direct provider, bring your own key -------------------------
# Skips the gateway entirely and calls the provider's own SDK. Use when you already
# have provider billing, or to avoid a middleman. The key must match the model prefix.
# AI_ROUTING=direct
# PAPERVINE_AI_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
#
# ---- Per-surface override (any profile) --------------------------------------
# Automation runs WRITE docs that land in Git, so they can justify a stronger model than
# the high-volume assistant. Unset = every surface uses PAPERVINE_AI_MODEL.
# NOTE: on the gateway's FREE tier, sonnet-class models are rate-limited — this override
# needs paid gateway credits or BYOK to run reliably.
# PAPERVINE_AI_MODEL_AUTOMATIONS=anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6

# --- Automations executor (SPEC §10.2/§18) -----------------------------------
# Trigger.dev environment secret key (dashboard → Project → API keys; tr_dev_… for
# local dev). The app uses it to enqueue automation runs and register cron schedules;
# the worker itself (`npx trigger.dev dev --env-file .env.local`) authenticates via the
# CLI login instead. Unset → automations degrade cleanly: the page shows "Executor not
# configured", nothing enqueues, and no other surface is affected.
# TRIGGER_SECRET_KEY=
#
# The executor is DEPLOYED separately from the app. This key only lets the app enqueue;
# the tasks (automation-run, automation-cron) run on Trigger.dev's cloud and must be
# published there with `npx trigger.dev deploy`. Prod does this automatically on every
# push to main (CI `deploy-trigger` job, gated on the tests) — set a TRIGGER_ACCESS_TOKEN
# repo secret (dashboard → Personal Access Tokens) to enable it. Locally, `npx trigger.dev
# dev` runs the tasks from your machine; no deploy needed. Skipping the deploy is why a
# prod run sits at "Pending version": enqueued, but no cloud version has the task yet.
#
# The deployed tasks run in Trigger.dev's cloud and DO NOT inherit the app's env — they
# need their OWN copy of every server var they touch: DATABASE_URL (the same migrated DB
# the app uses), the S3_* draft-buffer creds, the AI route (AI_ROUTING + AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY
# or PAPERVINE_AI_MODEL + a provider key), and the GITHUB_APP_* keys (to mint tokens for
# commits/PRs and code-change reads). A missing one fails a run on its first DB query. Set
# these in the Trigger.dev dashboard (prod environment → Environment Variables), OR let the
# deploy sync them from Vercel automatically: set VERCEL_ACCESS_TOKEN + VERCEL_PROJECT_ID
# (+ VERCEL_TEAM_ID for team projects) in the CI deploy step, and trigger.config.ts mirrors
# Vercel's env into the matching Trigger environment on every deploy. These are DEPLOY-time
# CI/dashboard secrets, not app runtime vars — they don't belong in this file.

# --- Private repos (SPEC §3) -------------------------------------------------
# Encrypts the per-site GitHub token stored for private repos (AES-256-GCM). Required
# only if you connect a private repo; without it, private connects error clearly.
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
PAPERVINE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- GitHub App (SPEC §3 — push auto-sync + private repos) -------------------
# Optional but recommended. Registering a GitHub App unlocks (a) push webhooks that
# auto-sync a site when its repo is pushed, and (b) private-repo access without users
# pasting a PAT (the App mints short-lived installation tokens). Unset → these features
# are simply off; public repos and the per-site PAT path still work.
# Register at https://github.com/settings/apps/new with:
#   • Permissions: Repository contents → Read-only (+ Metadata: Read, default).
#   • Subscribe to events: Push.
#   • Webhook URL:  https://papervine.io/api/github/webhook   (the APEX host — middleware
#     passes /api/ through ungated there; the app host would redirect GitHub to /login).
#   • Setup URL:    https://app.papervine.io/api/github/setup  (the APP host — it needs the
#     session cookie to tie the install to an org). Check "Redirect on update".
#   • Webhook secret: generate one (openssl rand -hex 32) and put it below.
# GITHUB_APP_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_SLUG=                 # the app's URL slug, for the "Install" button
# GITHUB_APP_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=          # the generated .pem — newlines as literal \n on one line

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Default auth for *public* repo connect/sync — raises the GitHub REST rate limit.
# Private repos use a per-site token from the connect form instead (encrypted at rest).
# GITHUB_TOKEN=
# Override the local single-tenant content dir served at the apex (defaults to ./content).
# PAPERVINE_CONTENT=
# Model for all AI surfaces (assistant, editor agent, automation runs), as a
# "provider/model" id. The assistant is AGENTIC (several model calls per question), and
# the gateway's FREE tier rate-limits the cheap Google/OpenAI models (they fail
# mid-conversation) and outright BLOCKS the newest ones. The active line is the best
# that works on the free tier today; the commented one is what to switch to once the
# gateway has PAID credits or a BYOK provider key, no code change needed.
#
# Best model the FREE tier reliably runs for our use case (also the code default):
PAPERVINE_AI_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5
# Cheaper (~10×) + newer + fast, but the free tier BLOCKS it — enable with paid/BYOK:
# PAPERVINE_AI_MODEL=google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite
# Automation runs WRITE docs that land in Git — override them to a stronger writer while
# the high-volume assistant stays on the cheap default. NOTE: sonnet-class models are
# rate-limited on the gateway FREE tier ("Free tier requests on this model are
# rate-limited") — this override needs paid gateway credits or BYOK to run reliably.
# PAPERVINE_AI_MODEL_AUTOMATIONS=anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6
# Custom domains (SPEC §2): attach customer hosts (docs.example.com) to the Vercel project
# so a per-host TLS cert issues. Unset locally/CI → Domain setup degrades to a DNS-only
# live check. Token: vercel.com/account/tokens; TEAM_ID only for team-owned projects.
# VERCEL_TOKEN=
# VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=
# VERCEL_TEAM_ID=
# Branded host customers CNAME their custom domain at (e.g. cname.yourdocs.com), a record
# in YOUR zone that CNAMEs to your provider's edge (cname.vercel-dns.com). Stable contract:
# move providers by re-pointing this one record, not every customer's DNS (SPEC §2 Phase 2).
# Unset → falls back to the raw Vercel edge (if VERCEL_* set) or the platform apex.
# CUSTOM_DOMAIN_CNAME_TARGET=cname.yourdocs.com
# Shared secret for the cron-driven reconcile loop (SPEC §2 — drains domain_removal
# tombstones so a custom domain is always eventually detached). Vercel Cron sends it as
# `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`; the /api/reconcile/domains route requires it in
# production (without it the route 401s and the retry safety net never runs — the inline
# detach still works). Generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32`. Unset locally (dev allows
# the loop to be run by hand).
# CRON_SECRET=

# Stripe billing (SPEC §10 Billing). Unset → billing degrades gracefully: checkout/portal
# actions return "billing not configured", the webhook 503s, and every org resolves by
# its DB state alone (new orgs still get the 30-day trial; nothing can be purchased).
# Setup: put the secret key here, then `npm run billing:sync && npm run billing:publish`
# to create the Products/Prices in whatever mode the key selects (sk_test_/sk_live_ —
# each environment's DB carries its own mode's ids). The webhook secret comes from the
# Stripe dashboard webhook endpoint (prod: /api/webhooks/stripe) or `stripe listen` (dev).
#
# LOCAL DEV — the whole loop (verified working 2026-07-17, test mode):
#   1. STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...  (from dashboard → Developers → API keys, Test mode)
#   2. stripe listen --api-key $STRIPE_SECRET_KEY --forward-to localhost:PORT/api/webhooks/stripe
#      ⚠️ GOTCHA: pass --api-key so the forwarder watches the SAME Stripe account the app
#      key belongs to. `stripe login`'s DEFAULT account can differ from your key's account —
#      if it does, checkout/subscription webhooks fire in the app's account but `stripe
#      listen` (default account) never sees them, and nothing mirrors. Copy the whsec it
#      prints → STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET below, then (re)start the dev server.
#   3. npm run billing:sync && npm run billing:publish
#   4. Settings → Billing → pick a plan → Stripe Checkout → test card 4242 4242 4242 4242.
#      (Stripe blocks BOT clicks on the hosted Checkout — complete it in a real browser.)
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
